select m.id, 1, $L$From Scripts to a Framework$L$, $L$16 minute read$L$, $L$A handful of specs needs no architecture; a few hundred does. This lesson is about recognising when an unstructured suite starts to cost you, and what "structure" actually buys — before you reach for any specific pattern.$L$, array[$L$Recognise the symptoms of an unstructured suite as it grows$L$, $L$Explain what structure buys a team and what it costs$L$, $L$Decide when to introduce structure rather than adding it up front$L$], $L$## Scripts are fine, until they are not
Your first ten Playwright specs do not need a framework. Each one navigates, acts and asserts, and you can read the whole suite in an afternoon. Reaching for page objects, fixtures and a folder taxonomy on day one is itself a form of over-engineering — you are paying for structure before you have the problem it solves.

The trouble is that suites grow, and they grow unevenly. What starts as ten readable specs becomes two hundred, written by five people over eighteen months, and the cracks show.

## The symptoms of an unstructured suite
You will recognise a suite that has outgrown its shape by how it *feels* to work in:

- **Shotgun edits.** A login form gains a "remember me" checkbox and forty specs need touching, because each one drives the login form directly.
- **Copy-paste drift.** The same flow is automated three slightly different ways because three people wrote it independently and none could find the others' version.
- **Mystery selectors.** A brittle CSS selector is duplicated across dozens of files; when it breaks, you fix it in some places and miss others.
- **Slow onboarding.** A new engineer cannot tell where a test for feature X should live, or whether one already exists.
- **Fear.** People stop refactoring the suite because they cannot predict what a change will break.

None of these is a Playwright problem. They are the ordinary consequences of a codebase with no agreed shape — and a test suite is a codebase.

## What structure actually buys
Structure is not an end in itself. It buys you exactly three things:

1. **One place to change.** When a behaviour changes in the product, you want to change it in one place in the suite, not forty.
2. **Findability.** A newcomer can predict where something lives and whether it already exists.
3. **Confidence to refactor.** Clear seams let you change internals without fear.

Everything in this module — page objects, fixtures, components, folder conventions — is a means to those ends. If a pattern does not buy you one of them, you do not need it yet.

## Structure has a cost
Every abstraction is a layer someone must learn, navigate and maintain. A page object is a file to open before you can read a test; a fixture is indirection between "the test asks for a user" and "a user gets created". These costs are worth paying when they buy you the three benefits above, and pure overhead when they do not. The whole skill of framework design is spending that cost deliberately.

## Key takeaway
A test suite is a codebase, and like any codebase it earns structure as it grows — introduce it in response to real pain (shotgun edits, drift, fear), not pre-emptively, and only where it buys you one place to change, findability, or the confidence to refactor.$L$, $L$The same login flow, unstructured and then with one seam extracted. First, the shape that does not scale — every spec drives the form directly:

~~~ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('user sees their dashboard after logging in', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('user@example.com');
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('secret');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Dashboard' })).toBeVisible();
});
~~~

Written once, this is perfectly clear. Written forty times, it means forty edits when the form changes. The first structural move is simply to name the flow in one place:

~~~ts
// support/login.ts
import { Page, expect } from '@playwright/test';

export async function loginAs(page: Page, email: string, password: string) {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(email);
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill(password);
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Dashboard' })).toBeVisible();
}
~~~

~~~ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { loginAs } from '../support/login';

test('user sees their dashboard after logging in', async ({ page }) => {
  await loginAs(page, 'user@example.com', 'secret');
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Welcome back' })).toBeVisible();
});
~~~

Notice this is not yet a page object or a fixture — just a well-named function. It already buys you one place to change and something a newcomer can find. That is often all the structure a flow needs; the later lessons only add more when this stops being enough.$L$, $L$- Building a full framework — base classes, page objects, config layers — before you have written enough tests to know what hurts
- Leaving a suite completely flat long after the shotgun edits and drift have become daily friction
- Extracting a helper for something used exactly once, so the indirection costs more than it saves
- Treating "we have a framework" as the goal, rather than "changes are cheap and tests are findable"$L$, $L$On a delivery team, let pain drive structure. The honest signal that a suite needs a seam is the third time you copy-paste the same flow, or the first change that forces you to edit a dozen specs. When that happens, extract the one thing that hurt — not a speculative architecture for everything you might one day need. A framework that grows out of real friction fits the team; one designed up front usually fits an imagined team that never shows up.$L$, $L$Take a small suite (yours or a sample of five to ten specs) and audit it for the symptoms in this lesson. List every place a single product change would force edits in more than one spec. Deliverable: a short table of "if X changed, N specs would need editing", and a one-line proposal for the single seam that would most reduce N.$L$, $L$Think of a suite you know. Is its current lack of structure costing the team anything today, or would adding structure now be solving a problem you do not yet have?$L$, $L$Name one concrete symptom that tells you a flat suite has outgrown its shape. (Answer: any of — the same product change forces edits across many specs, the same flow is copy-pasted several ways, a duplicated brittle selector, or people are afraid to refactor)$L$, array[$L$I can name the symptoms of an unstructured suite$L$, $L$I can explain what structure buys and what it costs$L$, $L$I introduce structure in response to real pain, not pre-emptively$L$]
