select m.id, 2, $L$Your First GitHub Actions Workflow$L$, $L$18 minute read$L$, $L$A GitHub Actions workflow is a YAML file in `.github/workflows/` that says when to run and what to do. This lesson builds a first working Playwright workflow from the ground up: triggers on push and pull request, a job on a runner, and the steps that check out the code, set up Node and run the tests.$L$, array[$L$Structure a workflow file with triggers, a job and steps$L$, $L$Trigger a run on push and pull_request, and understand the difference$L$, $L$Check out the repository and set up Node before running the suite$L$], $L$## Where a workflow lives
GitHub Actions reads any YAML file under `.github/workflows/` in your repository. Each file is one *workflow*. A workflow has three layers you will meet in every one you write:

- **Triggers** (`on:`) — the events that start a run.
- **Jobs** (`jobs:`) — one or more units of work, each on its own runner (a fresh virtual machine).
- **Steps** (`steps:`) — the ordered actions inside a job, run top to bottom.

## Triggers: push and pull_request
For a test suite you almost always want two triggers:

~~~yaml
on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:
~~~

`pull_request` runs the suite on every pull request and every update to it — this is your gate before code merges. `push` to `main` runs it again after merge, catching anything that only appears once changes are combined. Restricting `push` to `main` avoids running twice on branches that also have an open pull request.

## A job on a runner
A job declares the machine it runs on and the steps it performs:

~~~yaml
jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      # ...
~~~

`runs-on: ubuntu-latest` gives you a clean, GitHub-hosted Ubuntu machine. Every job starts from nothing — an empty runner with no code and no dependencies — which is exactly why the environment is repeatable. Your steps are responsible for putting everything in place.

## The essential steps
Because the runner starts empty, the first steps always build up the environment:

- **Check out the code** with `actions/checkout` — without this the runner has no repository.
- **Set up Node** with `actions/setup-node`, pinning a version so CI matches what the team develops on.
- **Install dependencies** with `npm ci` — the clean, lockfile-exact install meant for CI.
- **Run the tests** with `npx playwright test`.

Steps that use a published action have a `uses:`; steps that run a shell command have a `run:`. Pin actions to a major version (`actions/checkout@v4`) so a future release does not silently change your build.

## Reading a run
When the workflow runs, GitHub shows each step with a green tick or red X and its logs. A failing `npx playwright test` step fails the job, which fails the workflow, which shows red on the commit and pull request. That red is the raw material Lesson 5 turns into an enforced gate.

## Key takeaway
A workflow is triggers plus jobs plus steps in a YAML file under `.github/workflows/`; trigger on `push` to main and on `pull_request`, run on a clean runner, and always check out the code and set up Node before running the suite — because the runner starts with nothing.$L$, $L$A complete, working first workflow for a Playwright + TypeScript project. Save it as `.github/workflows/playwright.yml`:

~~~yaml
name: Playwright Tests

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:

jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Check out the repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Node
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 20

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: npm ci

      - name: Install Playwright browsers
        run: npx playwright install --with-deps

      - name: Run Playwright tests
        run: npx playwright test
~~~

Read it top to bottom the way the runner does. The workflow is named so it is identifiable in the Actions tab. It triggers on pushes to `main` and on every pull request. The single `test` job spins up a clean Ubuntu runner and then, step by step, assembles what it needs: it checks out your code, installs Node 20, runs `npm ci` for a lockfile-exact dependency install, installs the browsers Playwright drives (covered properly in Lesson 3), and finally runs the suite. If `npx playwright test` exits non-zero, that step goes red, the job fails, and the pull request shows a failing check. This is the smallest workflow that is genuinely useful — everything later in the module refines it.$L$, $L$- Forgetting `actions/checkout`, so the job runs against an empty runner and cannot find your tests
- Using `npm install` instead of `npm ci` in CI, giving a non-reproducible install that ignores the lockfile
- Leaving actions unpinned (`actions/checkout` with no `@v4`), so a new release can change the build under you
- Not pinning a Node version, so CI drifts from what developers run locally and "works on my machine" reappears$L$, $L$On a delivery team, keep the workflow file small and boring, and treat it as production code — reviewed in pull requests, changed deliberately, not tweaked directly on main at 5pm. Pin your actions and your Node version, use `npm ci`, and resist the urge to add clever conditional logic early. A workflow anyone on the team can read in thirty seconds is one they will maintain; a sprawling one with twenty steps and inline scripts becomes a thing only its author understands, and it rots the moment they move on.$L$, $L$Write a `.github/workflows/playwright.yml` that triggers on push to `main` and on `pull_request`, runs on `ubuntu-latest`, and has steps to check out the code, set up a pinned Node version, run `npm ci`, install Playwright browsers, and run the suite. Push it to a branch and open a pull request. Deliverable: the workflow file plus a screenshot or copy of the checks section on the pull request showing the run.$L$, $L$A job runs on a clean runner that starts with nothing. Why is that emptiness a feature rather than an inconvenience, and what class of bug does it protect you from?$L$, $L$In a GitHub Actions workflow, what are the three structural layers you define, from outermost to innermost? (Answer: triggers (on:), jobs, and steps)$L$, array[$L$I can write a workflow with triggers, a job and ordered steps$L$, $L$I can trigger on push and pull_request and explain the difference$L$, $L$I can check out the code and set up Node before running the suite$L$]
