select m.id, 6, $L$Sharding & Matrix in CI$L$, $L$19 minute read$L$, $L$As a suite grows, a single-runner CI job gets slow, and slow gates get bypassed. This lesson keeps feedback fast at scale: sharding splits the suite across parallel jobs, a matrix runs it across browsers, and Playwright's blob reporter lets you merge the fragments back into one coherent report.$L$, array[$L$Split a suite across parallel jobs with Playwright sharding and a job matrix$L$, $L$Run the suite across a browser matrix and understand the trade-offs$L$, $L$Merge blob reports from every shard into a single HTML report$L$], $L$## Why one runner stops being enough
Playwright already runs tests in parallel *within* a single machine using worker processes. But one runner has a ceiling — its CPU count — and a large suite eventually takes longer than a gate should. The next lever is horizontal: run the suite across *several* runners at once. Ten minutes on one machine becomes roughly two and a half minutes across four, and the gate stays fast as the suite grows.

## Sharding splits the suite
Playwright's built-in sharding divides the test suite into equal parts. `--shard=1/4` runs the first quarter of the tests, `2/4` the second, and so on:

~~~bash
npx playwright test --shard=1/4
~~~

Each shard is a subset of the whole suite, chosen deterministically by Playwright. Run four shards on four runners in parallel and you have executed the entire suite in a quarter of the wall-clock time.

## A matrix creates the parallel jobs
GitHub Actions' `strategy.matrix` runs the same job once per combination of values, in parallel. Combine it with sharding to fan the suite out across runners:

~~~yaml
jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    strategy:
      fail-fast: false
      matrix:
        shard: [1, 2, 3, 4]
    steps:
      - run: npx playwright test --shard=${{ matrix.shard }}/4
~~~

That single job definition becomes four parallel jobs, each running its quarter. `fail-fast: false` stops GitHub cancelling the other shards the moment one fails — you want the full picture of what failed across the whole suite, not just the first shard to go red.

## A browser matrix
The same mechanism runs the suite across browsers. Add a `browser` dimension and pass it through to Playwright's `--project`:

~~~yaml
    strategy:
      matrix:
        browser: [chromium, firefox, webkit]
    steps:
      - run: npx playwright test --project=${{ matrix.browser }}
~~~

Cross-browser coverage has real value — WebKit and Firefox catch issues Chromium does not — but it multiplies your job count and run time. Be deliberate: many teams gate on Chromium for speed and run the full browser matrix on a schedule rather than every pull request. Match the cost to where the risk actually is.

## Merging the shards into one report
Sharding creates a problem: each shard produces its own partial report, and four fragmentary HTML reports are useless. Playwright solves this with the **blob reporter**. Each shard emits a machine-readable blob report; a final job downloads all of them and merges them into one complete HTML report with `merge-reports`:

~~~bash
npx playwright merge-reports --reporter=html ./all-blob-reports
~~~

The pattern is: shards upload their blob reports as artifacts, then a dependent `merge` job (using `needs:`) downloads every shard's blob, runs `merge-reports`, and uploads the single unified HTML report. You get parallel speed *and* one coherent report to read.

## Fast at scale, still only as good as the tests
Sharding and matrices are, like caching, about keeping feedback fast so the gate stays trusted and unbypassed as the suite grows. They multiply how quickly the suite runs; they do not improve what it checks. Four shards of weak tests are weak tests delivered faster. The engineering here buys you *speed at scale* — genuinely valuable, because a slow gate is a bypassed gate — but the meaning of green is still set entirely by the quality of the tests inside the shards.

## Key takeaway
Shard the suite across a job matrix to keep a growing gate fast, add a browser matrix where cross-browser risk justifies the cost, and merge each shard's blob report into one HTML report with `merge-reports` — remembering that parallelism buys speed, not test quality.$L$, $L$A complete sharded workflow with a merge job: the suite runs across four parallel shards, each uploads a blob report, and a final job merges them into one HTML report.

~~~yaml
name: Playwright Tests

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:

jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    strategy:
      fail-fast: false                 # let every shard finish so you see all failures
      matrix:
        shard: [1, 2, 3, 4]
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 20
          cache: npm
      - run: npm ci
      - run: npx playwright install --with-deps
      - name: Run shard ${{ matrix.shard }} of 4
        run: npx playwright test --shard=${{ matrix.shard }}/4
      - name: Upload this shard's blob report
        if: ${{ !cancelled() }}
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: blob-report-${{ matrix.shard }}
          path: blob-report/
          retention-days: 1

  merge-report:
    needs: [test]                       # runs after all shards finish
    if: ${{ !cancelled() }}
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: 20
          cache: npm
      - run: npm ci
      - name: Download all shard blob reports
        uses: actions/download-artifact@v4
        with:
          path: all-blob-reports
          pattern: blob-report-*
          merge-multiple: true
      - name: Merge into one HTML report
        run: npx playwright merge-reports --reporter=html ./all-blob-reports
      - name: Upload the merged report
        uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with:
          name: playwright-report
          path: playwright-report/
          retention-days: 7
~~~

To make the shards emit blob reports, configure the reporter in `playwright.config.ts` so it uses blob on CI:

~~~ts
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  reporter: process.env.CI ? 'blob' : 'html',
});
~~~

Read it as two stages. The `test` job is a matrix of four shards running in parallel; each runs `--shard=N/4` and uploads its `blob-report/` as a distinctly named artifact. `fail-fast: false` keeps the other shards running when one fails, so a single red shard does not hide failures in the others. The `merge-report` job `needs` the test job, so it waits for all four shards, downloads every blob report into one folder, and runs `merge-reports --reporter=html` to stitch them into a single browsable report — the one artifact a human actually opens. The result: the wall-clock cost of a quarter-sized suite, with one coherent report instead of four fragments.$L$, $L$- Sharding without merging, leaving four partial reports that nobody can make sense of
- Leaving `fail-fast` at its default, so the first failing shard cancels the rest and hides the full set of failures
- Running the whole browser matrix on every pull request, tripling run time and cost for coverage the gate rarely needs at that moment
- Configuring the HTML reporter on the shards instead of the blob reporter, so there is nothing structured to merge$L$, $L$On a delivery team, add sharding when — and only when — the single-runner suite has grown slow enough to threaten the gate's speed; do not shard a two-minute suite for the sake of it, as the merge job and artifact plumbing add their own complexity. When you do shard, pick the shard count from the actual run time (aim to bring the gate back under a few minutes) and always add the merge job so reviewers still get one report. Keep the full browser matrix off the per-pull-request gate unless cross-browser bugs are a real and recurring problem for your product; run it nightly instead, where its cost buys coverage without slowing every merge.$L$, $L$Convert a single-job Playwright workflow into a sharded one: a matrix of at least two shards with `fail-fast: false`, each uploading its blob report, plus a `merge-report` job that `needs` the test job and produces one merged HTML report. Set the reporter to `blob` under CI. Run it and confirm you get a single unified report from the parallel shards. Deliverable: the sharded workflow, the config change, and a note on the run-time difference versus the single-job version.$L$, $L$Sharding made your gate four times faster but changed nothing about what the tests check. Given that, why is that speed still genuinely worth engineering for — and what would it be a mistake to claim it had improved?$L$, $L$After sharding a suite across four parallel jobs, how do you get a single coherent HTML report? (Answer: have each shard emit a blob report, then run npx playwright merge-reports --reporter=html on all the collected blobs in a final job)$L$, array[$L$I can split a suite across parallel jobs with Playwright sharding and a job matrix$L$, $L$I can run a browser matrix and reason about when its cost is justified$L$, $L$I can merge blob reports from every shard into one HTML report$L$]
